  "formatted_output": "\n\n# Near-Term (0\u20132 years)\n\n### Strategic Idea 1: Comprehensive Semiconductor Supply Chain Mapping & Risk Assessment\n**Summary:** To quickly identify critical dependencies and potential choke points in current semiconductor supply chains, enabling data-driven decisions for immediate risk mitigation and strategic planning. This provides an essential baseline for all future resilience efforts for non-aligned economies.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. Establish a cross-governmental task force: -- **Priority:** High\n2. Map existing semiconductor demand and supply sources: -- **Priority:** High\n3. Conduct vulnerability assessments: -- **Priority:** High\n4. Develop a real-time monitoring dashboard: -- **Priority:** Medium\n5. Formulate initial contingency plans: -- **Priority:** High\n\n### Strategic Idea 2: Diplomatic Engagement & Regional Partnership Strengthening\n**Summary:** To leverage non-alignment by fostering stronger bilateral and multilateral ties with other non-aligned nations and relevant blocs, diversifying diplomatic channels and exploring collective resilience mechanisms for semiconductor access and knowledge sharing.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. Initiate bilateral dialogues with key semiconductor-producing nations: -- **Priority:** High\n2. Strengthen intra-regional semiconductor dialogues: -- **Priority:** Medium\n3. Explore \"trusted supplier\" agreements and frameworks: -- **Priority:** Medium\n\n# Medium-Term (2\u20135 years)\n\n### Strategic Idea 1: Targeted Domestic Capability Development & Investment Incentives\n**Summary:** To strategically develop domestic capabilities in specific, achievable segments of the semiconductor value chain (e.g., design, assembly, testing, legacy node manufacturing) by attracting investment and fostering local talent, reducing critical external dependencies. This builds foundational resilience.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. Identify strategic niche areas for domestic production: -- **Priority:** High\n2. Design and implement attractive investment incentive packages: -- **Priority:** High\n3. Establish dedicated semiconductor talent development programs: -- **Priority:** Medium\n4. Facilitate public-private partnerships: -- **Priority:** High\n5. Develop a national semiconductor R&D roadmap: -- **Priority:** Medium\n\n### Strategic Idea 2: Regional Supply Chain Diversification & Infrastructure Upgrade\n**Summary:** To actively diversify sourcing away from single-bloc dependencies by fostering new regional partnerships and upgrading logistical and digital infrastructure to support a more resilient and distributed semiconductor supply network, enhancing regional self-reliance.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. Negotiate regional trade agreements focused on critical technologies: -- **Priority:** High\n2. Invest in resilient logistics and customs infrastructure: -- **Priority:** Medium\n3. Establish strategic semiconductor material reserves: -- **Priority:** High\n\n# Long-Term (5\u201310 years)\n\n### Strategic Idea 1: Towards Strategic Semiconductor Autonomy & Global Niche Leadership\n**Summary:** To build a robust, self-sufficient, and globally competitive domestic semiconductor ecosystem in chosen strategic areas, aiming for technological leadership and reducing long-term vulnerability to geopolitical shifts. This involves fostering deep innovation and attracting leading global talent.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. Develop a national \"Mega-Fab\" or advanced R&D institute: -- **Priority:** High\n2. Create a comprehensive intellectual property (IP) development strategy: -- **Priority:** Medium\n3. Attract and retain top-tier global semiconductor talent: -- **Priority:** High\n4. Foster a vibrant semiconductor startup ecosystem: -- **Priority:** Medium\n5. Influence international standards and governance: -- **Priority:** Low\n\n### Strategic Idea 2: Integrated Digital Economy & Critical Infrastructure Security\n**Summary:** To ensure the long-term security and resilience of the national digital economy and critical infrastructure by embedding robust semiconductor supply chain considerations into all strategic planning, fostering a culture of cyber-physical security and reducing systemic vulnerabilities.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. Mandate \"chip-to-system\" security protocols: -- **Priority:** High\n2. Integrate semiconductor resilience into national defense and economic planning: -- **Priority:** High\n3. Develop sovereign cloud and data center capabilities: -- **Priority:** Medium\n\n",
  "raw_llm_response": "The global semiconductor supply chain faces significant restructuring due to escalating strategic competition between major economic blocs (US/EU vs. China/Russia). Non-aligned regional economies such as India, Brazil, and ASEAN nations need proactive, multi-horizon strategies to build resilience, ensure access to critical components, and potentially leverage this geopolitical shift for their own economic and technological advancement by 2030.\n\n### Near-Term (0\u20132 Years) \u2013 Quick Wins & Urgent Needs\n\n#### Strategic Idea 1: Comprehensive Semiconductor Supply Chain Mapping & Risk Assessment\n**Summary:** To quickly identify critical dependencies and potential choke points in current semiconductor supply chains, enabling data-driven decisions for immediate risk mitigation and strategic planning. This provides an essential baseline for all future resilience efforts for non-aligned economies.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1.  **Establish a cross-governmental task force:** Convene experts from commerce, technology, defense, and foreign affairs to lead the mapping effort for critical semiconductor components. -- **Priority:** High\n2.  **Map existing semiconductor demand and supply sources:** Identify all critical chips, their global suppliers, manufacturing locations, and transit routes relevant to the national economy. -- **Priority:** High\n3.  **Conduct vulnerability assessments:** Analyze identified supply chains for geopolitical, logistical, and single-point-of-failure risks, including potential impacts of trade restrictions. -- **Priority:** High\n4.  **Develop a real-time monitoring dashboard:** Implement tools to track global semiconductor market trends, geopolitical shifts, and potential disruptions to critical supply lines. -- **Priority:** Medium\n5.  **Formulate initial contingency plans:** Outline immediate responses for identified high-risk supply chain disruptions, such as alternative sourcing options and emergency procurement protocols. -- **Priority:** High\n\n#### Strategic Idea 2: Diplomatic Engagement & Regional Partnership Strengthening\n**Summary:** To leverage non-alignment by fostering stronger bilateral and multilateral ties with other non-aligned nations and relevant blocs, diversifying diplomatic channels and exploring collective resilience mechanisms for semiconductor access and knowledge sharing.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1.  **Initiate bilateral dialogues with key semiconductor-producing nations:** Engage with diverse suppliers (e.g., Taiwan, South Korea, Japan, emerging players) to explore direct supply agreements and technology transfer opportunities. -- **Priority:** High\n2.  **Strengthen intra-regional semiconductor dialogues:** Host forums within ASEAN, MERCOSUR, or with India to share insights, coordinate early resilience strategies, and identify common vulnerabilities. -- **Priority:** Medium\n3.  **Explore \"trusted supplier\" agreements and frameworks:** Identify and engage with non-aligned or neutral suppliers for critical but non-cutting-edge semiconductor components to diversify procurement. -- **Priority:** Medium\n\n### Medium-Term (2\u20135 Years) \u2013 Strategic Build-Out\n\n#### Strategic Idea 1: Targeted Domestic Capability Development & Investment Incentives\n**Summary:** To strategically develop domestic capabilities in specific, achievable segments of the semiconductor value chain (e.g., design, assembly, testing, legacy node manufacturing) by attracting investment and fostering local talent, reducing critical external dependencies. This builds foundational resilience.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1.  **Identify strategic niche areas for domestic production:** Based on near-term assessments, select specific semiconductor segments (e.g., automotive chips, power management ICs, advanced packaging) where domestic entry is feasible and impactful. -- **Priority:** High\n2.  **Design and implement attractive investment incentive packages:** Offer competitive tax breaks, subsidies, land grants, and streamlined regulatory processes for semiconductor manufacturing and R&D facilities. -- **Priority:** High\n3.  **Establish dedicated semiconductor talent development programs:** Partner with universities and vocational schools to create specialized courses, scholarships, and internships in chip design, manufacturing, and materials science. -- **Priority:** Medium\n4.  **Facilitate public-private partnerships:** Co-invest with domestic and international companies in pilot projects for niche semiconductor manufacturing or advanced packaging to de-risk investments. -- **Priority:** High\n5.  **Develop a national semiconductor R&D roadmap:** Fund foundational research in materials science, advanced packaging, and emerging chip architectures relevant to national needs and identified niches. -- **Priority:** Medium\n\n#### Strategic Idea 2: Regional Supply Chain Diversification & Infrastructure Upgrade\n**Summary:** To actively diversify sourcing away from single-bloc dependencies by fostering new regional partnerships and upgrading logistical and digital infrastructure to support a more resilient and distributed semiconductor supply network, enhancing regional self-reliance.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1.  **Negotiate regional trade agreements focused on critical technologies:** Include provisions for preferential trade and investment in semiconductor components and manufacturing equipment among non-aligned partners. -- **Priority:** High\n2.  **Invest in resilient logistics and customs infrastructure:** Streamline cross-border movement of semiconductor goods and raw materials, including developing backup routes and expedited customs procedures. -- **Priority:** Medium\n3.  **Establish strategic semiconductor material reserves:** Secure national or regional stockpiles of critical raw materials (e.g., silicon, rare earths, specialized chemicals) required for chip manufacturing to mitigate supply shocks. -- **Priority:** High\n\n### Long-Term (5\u201310 Years) \u2013 Visionary & Transformational Strategies\n\n#### Strategic Idea 1: Towards Strategic Semiconductor Autonomy & Global Niche Leadership\n**Summary:** To build a robust, self-sufficient, and globally competitive domestic semiconductor ecosystem in chosen strategic areas, aiming for technological leadership and reducing long-term vulnerability to geopolitical shifts. This involves fostering deep innovation and attracting leading global talent.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1.  **Develop a national \"Mega-Fab\" or advanced R&D institute:** Establish a flagship national facility focused on cutting-edge research, prototyping, and potentially low-volume production of advanced nodes or specialized chips. -- **Priority:** High\n2.  **Create a comprehensive intellectual property (IP) development strategy:** Invest heavily in domestic chip design IP, patents, and open-source contributions to reduce licensing dependencies and foster local innovation. -- **Priority:** Medium\n3.  **Attract and retain top-tier global semiconductor talent:** Implement aggressive immigration policies, research grants, and competitive compensation packages to draw leading engineers and scientists. -- **Priority:** High\n4.  **Foster a vibrant semiconductor startup ecosystem:** Provide seed funding, mentorship, and access to testing facilities for innovative ventures in chip design, AI hardware, and quantum computing. -- **Priority:** Medium\n5.  **Influence international standards and governance:** Actively participate in global semiconductor consortia and standards bodies to shape future technological directions and ensure equitable access for non-aligned nations. -- **Priority:** Low\n\n#### Strategic Idea 2: Integrated Digital Economy & Critical Infrastructure Security\n**Summary:** To ensure the long-term security and resilience of the national digital economy and critical infrastructure by embedding robust semiconductor supply chain considerations into all strategic planning, fostering a culture of cyber-physical security and reducing systemic vulnerabilities.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1.  **Mandate \"chip-to-system\" security protocols:** Implement national standards for hardware security, traceability, and anti-tampering measures across critical infrastructure components. -- **Priority:** High\n2.  **Integrate semiconductor resilience into national defense and economic planning:** Embed supply chain security as a core pillar of national security doctrines and industrial policies, with regular stress tests. -- **Priority:** High\n3.  **Develop sovereign cloud and data center capabilities:** Reduce reliance on foreign-controlled digital infrastructure, using domestically produced or regionally sourced hardware where possible to enhance data sovereignty. -- **Priority:** Medium",
  "token_usage": 6156
}

